1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:02,936
A Rubik's Cube has twelve edge pieces.

2
00:00:03,086 --> 00:00:07,274
Label their positions and a rearrangement
gives a permutation of twelve letters.

3
00:00:07,424 --> 00:00:10,431
Herbert Kociemba asked David Joyner a
storage question.

4
00:00:10,581 --> 00:00:14,633
Could every edge permutation be specified
by two subgroup coordinates, with one

5
00:00:14,633 --> 00:00:17,697
coordinate coming from the symmetric
group on eight letters?

6
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:23,532
An exact factorization means that every
element is a product of one element from

7
00:00:23,532 --> 00:00:26,154
H and one from K, uniquely in that order.

8
00:00:26,304 --> 00:00:30,803
If two products agree, rearranging puts
the difference of the H coordinates in

9
00:00:30,803 --> 00:00:31,680
both subgroups.

10
00:00:31,830 --> 00:00:35,231
A trivial intersection forces that
difference to be the identity.

11
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:40,944
In the symmetric group on three letters,
take H generated by the swap of one and

12
00:00:40,944 --> 00:00:41,170
two.

13
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:43,387
Take K generated by the three-cycle.

14
00:00:43,537 --> 00:00:47,055
Two choices in H and three in K give six
distinct products.

15
00:00:47,205 --> 00:00:51,083
Each cell of the grid identifies one
permutation, and the row and column

16
00:00:51,083 --> 00:00:52,395
recover its coordinates.

17
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:56,709
Find the swap of one and three in the
grid.

18
00:00:56,859 --> 00:01:01,511
First apply the inverse three-cycle: one
goes to three, three to two, and two to

19
00:01:01,511 --> 00:01:01,747
one.

20
00:01:01,897 --> 00:01:03,766
Then swap the labels one and two.

21
00:01:03,916 --> 00:01:06,598
The composite swaps one and three and
fixes two.

22
00:01:06,748 --> 00:01:09,267
This is the paper's rightmost-first
convention.

23
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:17,183
In the standard embedding, the symmetric
group on m letters permutes the first m

24
00:01:17,183 --> 00:01:20,398
letters and fixes the last k, where k
equals n minus m.

25
00:01:20,548 --> 00:01:22,464
List those last letters in order.

26
00:01:22,614 --> 00:01:26,317
The entire stabilizer of this tuple is
exactly the standard subgroup.

27
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:33,967
Write a permutation as a complement
element followed on the right by a

28
00:01:33,967 --> 00:01:34,927
stabilizer element.

29
00:01:35,077 --> 00:01:39,525
The right factor fixes the base tuple, so
the left factor alone determines its

30
00:01:39,525 --> 00:01:39,872
image.

31
00:01:40,022 --> 00:01:44,144
If two complement elements send the base
tuple to the same target, their quotient

32
00:01:44,144 --> 00:01:46,256
lies in the intersection, and they agree.

33
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:52,632
For five letters, label the fixed pair
zero and one in the field of five

34
00:01:52,632 --> 00:01:53,159
elements.

35
00:01:53,309 --> 00:01:56,513
An affine map sends zero to b and one to
a plus b.

36
00:01:56,663 --> 00:02:01,710
So for target three, two, choose b equal
to three and a equal to four, doing

37
00:02:01,710 --> 00:02:03,258
arithmetic modulo five.

38
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:10,148
For any distinct target entries, their
difference gives a nonzero slope.

39
00:02:10,298 --> 00:02:14,026
The same reasoning works from any
starting pair, or we can go back through

40
00:02:14,026 --> 00:02:14,741
the base pair.

41
00:02:14,891 --> 00:02:16,671
This is sharp two-transitivity.

42
00:02:16,821 --> 00:02:21,383
Conversely, a unique mover to the target
leaves a residual permutation fixing the

43
00:02:21,383 --> 00:02:21,668
base.

44
00:02:21,818 --> 00:02:23,684
So regularity gives a complement.

45
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:27,057
For degrees above twelve there are four
families.

46
00:02:27,207 --> 00:02:29,808
A full cycle handles a single fixed
letter.

47
00:02:29,958 --> 00:02:34,649
At the other extreme, fixing all but two
images leaves two completions of opposite

48
00:02:34,649 --> 00:02:35,055
parity.

49
00:02:35,205 --> 00:02:36,830
Exactly one is even.

50
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:40,517
This makes the alternating group a
complement to the standard symmetric

51
00:02:40,517 --> 00:02:41,578
group on two letters.

52
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:46,835
Two fixed letters require a prime-power
degree, and the affine group supplies a

53
00:02:46,835 --> 00:02:47,449
complement.

54
00:02:47,599 --> 00:02:50,548
Three require the degree minus one to be
a prime power.

55
00:02:50,698 --> 00:02:55,113
The projective linear group acts on that
field with a point at infinity, sending

56
00:02:55,113 --> 00:02:57,908
any ordered triple to any other in
exactly one way.

57
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:04,591
Between sharp four-transitivity and the
alternating case, the extra groups are

58
00:03:04,591 --> 00:03:08,702
Mathieu eleven in degree eleven, and
Mathieu twelve in degree twelve, sharp on

59
00:03:08,702 --> 00:03:10,571
four and five letters respectively.

60
00:03:10,721 --> 00:03:14,195
The degree restrictions come from
classical results of Zassenhaus and

61
00:03:14,195 --> 00:03:17,670
Jordan, as cited through Cameron and
Dixon and Mortimer in the draft.

62
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:22,557
For twelve letters, the allowed values of
k are one, three, five, and ten.

63
00:03:22,707 --> 00:03:25,126
Eight movable letters would leave four
fixed letters.

64
00:03:25,276 --> 00:03:28,315
Sharp four-transitivity in this range
requires degree eleven.

65
00:03:28,465 --> 00:03:32,440
The generator scripts check specific
positive examples by orders and orbits.

66
00:03:32,590 --> 00:03:35,716
The impossibility in degree twelve
follows from classification.

67
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:39,046
The cube question has a three-coordinate
answer.

68
00:03:39,196 --> 00:03:42,203
Use the projective line over the field of
eleven elements.

69
00:03:42,353 --> 00:03:45,499
The group P is sharply transitive on
ordered triples.

70
00:03:45,649 --> 00:03:50,566
The cyclic group C fixes zero, one, and
infinity and cycles the remaining nine

71
00:03:50,566 --> 00:03:51,013
points.

72
00:03:51,163 --> 00:03:53,497
The final subgroup also fixes two.

73
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:56,767
Take g to be the swap of zero and one.

74
00:03:56,917 --> 00:04:00,609
Its first three images determine u, the
map one minus x.

75
00:04:00,759 --> 00:04:04,881
To find the C coordinate, pull the fourth
target, two, back through u.

76
00:04:05,031 --> 00:04:08,398
That gives ten, so use the eighth power
of the nine-cycle.

77
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:16,840
The residual factor h fixes the base
quadruple and swaps the displayed four

78
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:17,194
pairs.

79
00:04:17,344 --> 00:04:22,477
Watch the images of the base quadruple: h
fixes it, the cycle power moves two to

80
00:04:22,477 --> 00:04:26,051
ten, and u swaps zero and one and sends
ten back to two.

81
00:04:26,201 --> 00:04:28,558
On the remaining points the changes
cancel.

82
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:33,292
For any g, its first three images force
u.

83
00:04:33,442 --> 00:04:38,046
Its fourth image then forces the cycle
power, since the cycle acts regularly on

84
00:04:38,046 --> 00:04:38,755
nine points.

85
00:04:38,905 --> 00:04:42,197
The residual h is forced by
multiplication with the inverse.

86
00:04:42,347 --> 00:04:46,812
So the three indices have sizes thirteen
twenty, nine, and forty thousand three

87
00:04:46,812 --> 00:04:47,671
hundred twenty.

88
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:52,288
Now use the ordered pairs of distinct
field elements as vertices.

89
00:04:52,438 --> 00:04:55,248
Join two when exactly one coordinate
changes.

90
00:04:55,398 --> 00:04:58,788
All pairs in a common row or column are
adjacent in this layout.

91
00:04:58,938 --> 00:05:02,549
The base zero, one has six neighbors,
three for each coordinate.

92
00:05:02,699 --> 00:05:05,357
This is the arrangement graph T of five,
two.

93
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:11,426
Each pair b, a plus b has a unique affine
label, the map a x plus b.

94
00:05:11,576 --> 00:05:15,686
Keep the vertices in place and follow
just the six neighbors of the identity.

95
00:05:15,836 --> 00:05:18,193
The blue edges lead to maps fixing zero.

96
00:05:18,343 --> 00:05:20,630
The gold edges lead to maps fixing one.

97
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:22,521
Together they give the connection set.

98
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:27,905
Apply the translation x plus one to the
base and its six neighbors.

99
00:05:28,055 --> 00:05:32,586
Translation preserves agreement in each
coordinate, so it carries those edges to

100
00:05:32,586 --> 00:05:33,848
the edges at one, two.

101
00:05:33,998 --> 00:05:38,841
In group labels we moved from the
identity and d to x and x d, using left

102
00:05:38,841 --> 00:05:39,850
multiplication.

103
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:45,099
Every vertex has exactly one group label.

104
00:05:45,249 --> 00:05:49,725
At the vertex labeled x, all neighbors
therefore have labels x d, where d runs

105
00:05:49,725 --> 00:05:51,528
through the six displayed maps.

106
00:05:51,678 --> 00:05:53,366
This is the Cayley graph rule.

107
00:05:53,516 --> 00:05:57,616
The script checks the edge sets for this
example; the argument works for any

108
00:05:57,616 --> 00:05:59,694
regular action by graph automorphisms.

109
00:06:00,480 --> 00:06:04,323
In T of four, two, the two orders of each
pair have the same color.

110
00:06:04,473 --> 00:06:08,316
Forgetting order sends these two vertices
to a single two-element set.

111
00:06:08,466 --> 00:06:11,020
The resulting Johnson graph has six
vertices.

112
00:06:11,170 --> 00:06:15,645
Its edges join sets that share one
element, or equivalently, differ by one

113
00:06:15,645 --> 00:06:16,197
exchange.

114
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:21,661
Look at the edge from the set one, two to
the set one, three.

115
00:06:21,811 --> 00:06:25,666
In either ordering upstairs, replace the
two by three in its own slot.

116
00:06:25,816 --> 00:06:29,758
The same rule gives one lift at each
ordered vertex for every incident edge

117
00:06:29,758 --> 00:06:30,344
downstairs.

118
00:06:30,494 --> 00:06:32,908
The diagram highlights the two lifts.

119
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:38,721
Now merge the two orders in each fiber.

120
00:06:38,871 --> 00:06:41,402
The paired edges trace the same Johnson
edge.

121
00:06:41,552 --> 00:06:45,436
In general, a k-element set has k
factorial orders, and replacing the

122
00:06:45,436 --> 00:06:49,377
changing symbol in its own slot gives the
unique lift at every vertex.

123
00:06:49,527 --> 00:06:52,046
This is a k factorial fold graph
covering.

124
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:56,880
A complement always gives a Cayley
representation.

125
00:06:57,030 --> 00:06:59,795
But arrangement graphs also admit
coordinate permutations.

126
00:06:59,945 --> 00:07:03,961
Yin, Feng, Zhou, and Guo's
classification, as quoted in Draft Six,

127
00:07:03,961 --> 00:07:05,753
gives six extra Cayley cases.

128
00:07:05,903 --> 00:07:09,275
Their regular groups may use coordinate
permutations even when the natural

129
00:07:09,275 --> 00:07:11,723
symmetric group on the symbols contains
no complement.
